{"name":"Chrysolite","hex":"#8e9849","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"chrysolite","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Originally, any of various green-coloured gems; later specifically peridot."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/chrysolite"}],"entries":[{"title":"Peridot","blocks":[{"text":"Peridot, sometimes called chrysolite, is a yellow-green transparent variety of olivine, specifically the magnesium rich end member called forsterite. Peridot is one of the few gemstones that occur in only one color."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Forsterite-Olivine-tmu14a.jpg/330px-Forsterite-Olivine-tmu14a.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peridot"},{"title":"Topaz","blocks":[{"text":"Topaz is a silicate mineral made of aluminum and fluorine with the chemical formula Al2SiO4(F, OH)2. It is used as a gemstone in jewelry and other adornments. Common topaz in its natural state is colorless, though trace element impurities can make it pale blue or golden-brown to yellow-orange. Topaz is often treated with heat or radiation to make it a deep blue, reddish-orange, pale green, pink, or purple."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Topaze%2C_quartz_fum%C3%A9_1.jpg/330px-Topaze%2C_quartz_fum%C3%A9_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Topaz"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Atomic_structure_of_olivine_1.png/250px-Atomic_structure_of_olivine_1.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cc/Atomic_structure_of_olivine_1.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Atomic_structure_of_olivine_1.p